### Create Order Example

Source: https://github.com/paypal/paypal-typescript-server-sdk/blob/main/doc/controllers/orders.md

Demonstrates how to create a new order with capture intent. Includes handling of successful responses and various error types.

```typescript
const collect = {
  body: {
    intent: CheckoutPaymentIntent.Capture,
    purchaseUnits: [
      {
        amount: {
          currencyCode: 'currency_code6',
          value: 'value0',
        },
      }
    ],
  },
  prefer: 'return=minimal'
}

try {
  const response = await ordersController.createOrder(collect);

  // Extracting fully parsed response body.
  console.log(response.result);

  // Extracting response status code.
  console.log(response.statusCode);
  // Extracting response headers.
  console.log(response.headers);
  // Extracting response body of type `string | Stream`
  console.log(response.body);
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof ApiError) {
    // Extracting response error status code.
    console.log(error.statusCode);
    // Extracting response error headers.
    console.log(error.headers);
    // Extracting response error body of type `string | Stream`.
    console.log(error.body);
    if (error instanceof CustomError) {
      console.log(error.result);
    }
  }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Initialize Orders Controller

Source: https://github.com/paypal/paypal-typescript-server-sdk/blob/main/doc/controllers/orders.md

Instantiate the OrdersController with a client instance. This is typically done once at the application's setup.

```typescript
const ordersController = new OrdersController(client);
```

--------------------------------

### Initialize Payments Controller

Source: https://github.com/paypal/paypal-typescript-server-sdk/blob/main/doc/controllers/payments.md

Instantiate the PaymentsController with a client instance.

```typescript
const paymentsController = new PaymentsController(client);
```

--------------------------------

### Void Payment Example Usage

Source: https://github.com/paypal/paypal-typescript-server-sdk/blob/main/doc/controllers/payments.md

Demonstrates how to use the `voidPayment` method to void an authorized payment. Includes error handling for API errors and custom errors.

```typescript
const collect = {
  authorizationId: 'authorization_id8',
  prefer: 'return=minimal'
}

try {
  const response = await paymentsController.voidPayment(collect);

  // Extracting fully parsed response body.
  console.log(response.result);

  // Extracting response status code.
  console.log(response.statusCode);
  // Extracting response headers.
  console.log(response.headers);
  // Extracting response body of type `string | Stream`
  console.log(response.body);
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof ApiError) {
    // Extracting response error status code.
    console.log(error.statusCode);
    // Extracting response error headers.
    console.log(error.headers);
    // Extracting response error body of type `string | Stream`.
    console.log(error.body);
    if (error instanceof CustomError) {
      console.log(error.result);
    }
  }
}
```

### Orders Controller > Example Usage

Source: https://github.com/paypal/paypal-typescript-server-sdk/blob/main/doc/controllers/orders.md

When creating an order, the request body includes the payment intent and purchase units with currency code and value. The response provides the result, status code, headers, and body. Errors during order creation are caught as ApiErrors, which can be further inspected for status code, headers, body, and specific results if they are CustomErrors.

